  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Early week cooldown precedes wet/breezy \nThanksgiving Day \n\nComplex low pressure system moves up the East Coast today ahead\nof an approaching cold front from the Great Lakes/Ohio Valley.  \nModel guidance has generally consolidated and is largely\nagreeable on an eastward shift in the QPF axis much closer to \nthe Mid Atlantic coast or to the east of I-95. Precip probs have\ndecreased and shifted to the east in kind with little to no \nrain now expected to close out the weekend. \n\nThe cold front will move through early Monday behind the \ndeparting coastal low. This feature could generate some light\nrain particularly along the Allegheny Mtns. Surge of colder air\nspills into CPA behind the front triggering a brief period of \nrain and snow showers downwind of Lake Erie Monday night into \nearly Tuesday. Strong high pressure migrating eastward from the \nUpper Midwest will shut off the lake effect precip and bring \nimproving conditions by Tuesday night, setting up what should be\ncoldest night so far this season beneath the Beaver Supermoon. \nWe have high confidence in a noticeable early week cool down by \nTuesday with fcst highs 15 to 25 degrees lower than this \nweekend. Next week will certainly feel more like late November \nwith seasonably colder temperatures lasting through the \nThanksgiving holiday weekend. \n\nSpeaking of Thanksgiving, there is a growing widespread precip \nsignal to dampen the holiday as a strong storm moves up the East\nCoast. NBM/WPC rainfall generally ranges between 0.50 to 1 inch\nacross CPA. The Probabilistic Xi (Winter Storm Severity Index) \ncontinues to highlight some potential for minor winter weather \nimpacts resulting from a transition to elevation driven snow \nThursday night into Friday. Winds may also be a factor with \ntight gradient/strong easterly flow initially on Thursday \nfollowed by cold air advection westerlies as the storm moves out\non Black Friday. The late week NW flow regime could also \nproduce lake effect/upslope snow showers and post Thanksgiving \ntravel impacts over the Alleghenies.",
